\section{Diversity and Selection Analysis}\label{app:diversity}

\paragraph{Output diversity quantification.}
Table~\ref{tab:diversity} summarizes output diversity across configurations.
On SciERC, output diversity is substantial: Qwen produces 43\% unique outputs per instance (mean 3.5 distinct out of $N{=}8$), with per-sample F1 standard deviation of 0.144 and mean pairwise SJ of 0.703.
LLaMA shows similar diversity (39\% unique, F1 std 0.11).
On CoNLL, diversity collapses: 57\% (Qwen) to 88\% (LLaMA) of instances produce identical outputs across all $N$ samples, reflecting a ceiling effect where greedy F1 already exceeds 0.90.
WNUT-17 shows 55\% identical outputs, driven by the 52\% zero-F1 rate.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Output diversity across configurations ($N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$). ``Identical'' = fraction of instances with all $N$ outputs identical. ``Unique'' = mean fraction of distinct outputs per instance.}
\label{tab:diversity}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
Config & Identical & Unique \\
\midrule
Qwen/SciERC & 11\% & 43\% \\
LLaMA/SciERC & 18\% & 39\% \\
Qwen/CoNLL & 57\% & --- \\
LLaMA/CoNLL & 88\% & --- \\
WNUT-17 & 55\% & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Oracle-improvable fraction.}
Across SciERC configurations, 39--42\% of instances contain at least one sample with F1 exceeding greedy: Qwen NER 42.0\% (222/529, mean headroom $+$31.9~pp among improvable instances), Qwen RE 41.3\% (164/397, $+$45.3~pp), LLaMA NER 39.3\% (208/529, $+$33.7~pp).
On CoNLL, only 4--9\% of instances are improvable, confirming the ceiling effect.

\paragraph{Diversity-stratified selection gap.}
On the 44\% of Qwen SciERC instances with high diversity (${\geq}4$ unique outputs among 8 samples, $n{=}232$), oracle headroom reaches $+$16.3~pp, yet all consistency-based signals (SJ, FK, EM, Voting) select at or below greedy, and logprob---the only per-sample signal---achieves merely $+$0.8~pp.
LLaMA replicates this pattern: on its high-diversity subset ($n{=}186$), oracle headroom reaches $+$20.1~pp while consistency signals remain at or below greedy and logprob captures only $+$2.0~pp.
This rules out insufficient diversity as an explanation for the correlation-selection gap.

\paragraph{TOST equivalence testing.}
Table~\ref{tab:tost} reports two one-sided tests (TOST) results, testing whether each signal's selection F1 is equivalent to greedy within a bound $\delta$.
On NER ($n{=}529$), at $\delta{=}{\pm}2$~pp, SJ and logprob are confirmed equivalent to greedy (TOST $p{<}0.05$); at $\delta{=}{\pm}3$~pp, four of five signals pass; at $\delta{=}{\pm}5$~pp, all five pass.
On RE ($n{=}397$), the picture is starker: EM and Voting fail equivalence even at $\delta{=}{\pm}5$~pp due to systematic underperformance (mean $\Delta{=}{-}3.2$~pp and $-4.5$~pp respectively).

